<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ralph Moor</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1860">1860</a>–<a href="/years/1909">1909</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sir Ralph Moor was a British colonial administrator who served as commissioner of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> Coast <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">Protectorate</a> from 1896 and then as the first high commissioner of the Protectorate of Southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a> from 1900 to 1903. He organised the punitive expedition of 1897 that sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-city">Benin City</a> and ended the independence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ralph-moor.jpg" alt="Ralph Moor" width="320" height="349" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ralph_Denham_Rayment_Moor.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ralph Denham Rayment Moor was born on 31 July 1860 at The Lodge, Furneux Pelham, near Buntingford in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hertfordshire">Hertfordshire</a>, the son of William Henry Moor, a surgeon, and his wife Sarah Pears. Educated privately and meant for business, he worked for a time as a learner in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a> trade. On 26 October 1882 he entered the Royal Irish Constabulary as a cadet and rose to district inspector, but resigned on 9 February 1891 after involvement in a divorce case.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In March 1891 Moor became Commandant of Constabulary in the Oil Rivers Protectorate under the Consul-General, Sir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claude-maxwell-macdonald">Claude Maxwell MacDonald</a>. He was made vice-consul for the Oil Rivers district in July 1892 and acted as commissioner from 6 September 1892 to 15 February 1893. While acting consul during MacDonald's leave in 1894 he razed Brohemie. On 1 February 1896, when the district became the Niger Coast Protectorate, he was made its commissioner and consul-general, and consul for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cameroon">Cameroons</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bioko">Fernando Po</a>.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-expedition-of-1897">Benin Expedition of 1897</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oba-of-benin">Oba of Benin</a> was captured, deposed and exiled. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-city">Benin City</a> was plundered and more than 2,500 artefacts, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-bronzes">Benin Bronzes</a>, were shipped to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. Moor kept a Queen Idia mask, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a> bought after his death. As High Commissioner of Southern Nigeria from 1900 he laid the foundations of the new administration; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a> was abolished in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-aro-war">Anglo-Aro War</a> of 1901 and 1902 extended British control with new posts at Bende and Owerri.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Moor was made a Companion of the Order of St Michael and St George in 1895 and a Knight Commander in 1897. In 1898 he married Adrienne Shapland, the widow of J. Burns. His health failing, he retired on 1 October 1903, then advised Sir Alfred Lewis Jones on West African affairs and helped develop the British Cotton Growing Association. He was found dead at his home in Barnes on 14 September 1909, having taken poison. The coroner's jury heard that malarial fever since his return from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> had left him with severe insomnia.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ralph Moor” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under <b>Ralph Moor</b>, commissioner from 1896, an expedition in 1897 avenged the killing of acting consul James Phillips by sacking Benin City, ending the independence of the Benin Empire and seizing the Benin Bronzes.</blockquote><a href="/state/niger-coast-protectorate">Niger Coast Protectorate</a></li><li><blockquote>On 1 January 1900 Britain combined the Niger Coast Protectorate with the southern lands of the Royal Niger Company to form the Protectorate of Southern Nigeria, under the high commissioner <b>Ralph Moor</b>, with headquarters at Old Calabar.</blockquote><a href="/state/protectorate-of-southern-nigeria">Protectorate of Southern Nigeria</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Furneux Pelham, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Barnes, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.93,0.08&amp;year=1891" aria-label="Furneux Pelham on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4552 369 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ralph Moor"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5002 639h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5002 639h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.93,0.08&amp;year=1891">What was here in 1891? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
